wayland-server: hide the cursor when a client sets a NULL cursor surface

wl_pointer.set_cursor with a NULL surface means "hide the pointer image",
the request Wine's Wayland driver sends for SetCursor(NULL), ShowCursor(FALSE)
and a fully transparent cursor. HandleSetCursor() ignored it, so the last
cursor the client set (usually the arrow) stayed on top of every game that
draws its own cursor (Warcraft III, Diablo II...).

- set_cursor(NULL) sets the application cursor to B_CURSOR_ID_NO_CURSOR while
  one of the client's surfaces has the pointer.
- wl_pointer.leave to no surface restores the system default cursor: outside
  the client's surfaces (the window decorator, borders) the cursor is the
  compositor's, and the client sets its own again at the next enter.

--- a/HaikuSeat.cpp
+++ b/HaikuSeat.cpp
@@ -226,6 +226,15 @@
 		hook->fHotspot = BPoint(hotspot_x, hotspot_y);
 		surface->SetHook(hook);
 		hook->HandleCommit();
+	} else {
+		// A NULL surface hides the cursor (wl_pointer.set_cursor), e.g. a game
+		// that draws its own. Only while one of the client's surfaces has the
+		// pointer: elsewhere the cursor is not the client's to hide.
+		HaikuSurface *focus = fGlobal->fPointerFocus;
+		if (focus != NULL && focus->Client() == Client()) {
+			BCursor noCursor(B_CURSOR_ID_NO_CURSOR);
+			be_app->SetCursor(&noCursor, true);
+		}
 	}
 }
 
@@ -279,6 +288,12 @@
 				pointer->SendLeave(NextSerial(), fPointerFocus->ToResource());
 				pointer->SendFrame();
 			}
+			// Outside the client's surfaces (window borders, decorator) the
+			// cursor is the compositor's again: a cursor the client set (or
+			// hid) applies only until the pointer leaves, and the client sets
+			// it anew on the next enter.
+			if (surface == NULL)
+				be_app->SetCursor(B_CURSOR_SYSTEM_DEFAULT, true);
 			break;
 		case trackDrag:
 			fDataDevice->SendLeave();
